freedreno: a8xx does not get 128-wide waves, and upstream never gave it them.

Resident Evil Requiem hung its GPU on a `256x1x1` compute dispatch. vkd3d
breadcrumbs named the command; the shader dump showed a wave-aggregated
allocation loop — ballot for the active count, an exclusive scan for this lane's
index, one lane doing the `atomicAdd`, `WaveReadLaneFirst` to share the base
back, loop while the index is in range. Its exit depends on every lane agreeing
about the wave, so when they disagree the index stops advancing and the dispatch
never retires: `VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST`, then a critical section held by the thread
that noticed, then a frozen loading screen. **33 of the 2,867 shaders one session
compiled carry the same loop.**

**THE SHADER IS SIZE-AGNOSTIC, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** It hardcodes no wave
width; it is written against ballots and scans, which are width-independent by
contract. So the usual reading — "a game assuming a 64-wide desktop wave" — is
not this. The driver is wrong, not the title.

`a8xx_base` sets both `supports_double_threadsize` and `has_dual_wave_dispatch`.
`freedreno_dev_info.h:235-241` says they are alternatives, not companions:

    /* Dual-wave dispatch replaces THREAD128 (supports_double_threadsize)
     * on newer devices.
     *
     * This has some implications for marking join-points (jp), because
     * while a wave may not diverge, a pair of waves might, for example
     * subgroup-ops.
     */

The second paragraph is the bug, written down in the tree before anyone hit it:
**a pair of waves can diverge where a single wave cannot, and subgroup ops are
named as the case.** That is a ballot-driven loop across a dual-wave pair.

**Upstream does not enable this.** `supports_double_threadsize` was flipped to
True for a8xx by `7e0c63c3f51` ("freedreno/common: update a8xx family configs",
2026-04-15) on the `turnip/gen8` fork this component is built from — a one-line
change inside a commit about GMEM sizes and tile alignment, whose message never
mentions subgroups. Upstream added `has_dual_wave_dispatch = True` to the same
struct and deliberately left the other False.

**Restricting to 64 is not a reduction.** `threadsize_base` defaults to 64
(`freedreno_dev_info.py:108`) and a8xx does not override it, so 64 is the native
width and 128 is the opt-in. `minSubgroupSize` is already 64 unconditionally
(`tu_device.cc:1111`). What this removes is a mode the chip was never configured
for here.

**Watched on the device.** Reaching the same state through the drirc route —
`tu_restrict_subgroup_size_64=true`, which is defined as the negation of this
very flag (`tu_device.cc:1796`) — the title survived 600 s with `vr -4` at zero
and five swapchains created, **with no shader overrides at all**. Without it, it
never reached a loading screen.

A per-shader `VKD3D_SHADER_OVERRIDE` was built first, and it worked: all 33 loops
rewritten to a per-lane `atomicAdd`, each assembled and `spirv-val` clean. It is
the wrong fix and is not kept. It covers only shaders already compiled, rewrites
the game's own algorithm, needs redoing after any game update, and forces
`pipeline_library_ignore_spirv`, which takes the PSO cache out of the picture.

Preferred over the drirc option for the same reason: the option is a per-title
workaround for a defect that is not per-title. This is the defect.

